wifi: ath11k: wake a TXQ without an atomic when its push is pending

mac80211 wakes the TXQ of every frame it queues, from whichever CPU sent
it, and ath11k_mac_op_wake_tx_queue() then sets the AC's bit in
txq_woken and calls queue_work() for the push work, whose
test_and_set_bit() of the pending bit is a second read-modify-write. All
transmitting CPUs share both lines. In a profile of routed downlink at
900 Mbit/s (cand18, one run) the instruction after queue_work_on()'s
interrupt restore takes 1.07 % of all samples.

Both operations change nothing while the AC is marked and the work has
not started yet, which is the common case: the work runs after the poll
that queued the frames. Read the mark and the pending bit first, after a
full barrier that orders the frame mac80211 queued before them, and set
or queue only what is missing. The work clears its pending bit, issues a
full barrier and then takes the marks with xchg(), so a wake that sees
the work pending has its mark read by that run, and a wake that sees a
mark already set has its frame dequeued by the run that takes the mark.

The module parameter txq_wake_check (default on) is a test switch for the
A/B and is removed before this goes upstream.

Signed-off-by: Julius Bairaktaris <julius@bairaktaris.de>
--- a/drivers/net/wireless/ath/ath11k/mac.c
+++ b/drivers/net/wireless/ath/ath11k/mac.c
@@ -10379,12 +10379,44 @@ static void ath11k_mac_txq_work(struct w
 	rcu_read_unlock();
 }
 
+/* TEST-ONLY switch for the A/B of the wake that skips the atomics when the
+ * push is already due; dropped before this is upstreamed.
+ */
+static bool ath11k_txq_wake_check = true;
+module_param_named(txq_wake_check, ath11k_txq_wake_check, bool, 0644);
+MODULE_PARM_DESC(txq_wake_check,
+		 "Wake a TXQ without an atomic when its AC is marked and its push work pending (default: on)");
+
 static void ath11k_mac_op_wake_tx_queue(struct ieee80211_hw *hw,
 					struct ieee80211_txq *txq)
 {
 	struct ath11k *ar = hw->priv;
 
-	set_bit(txq->ac, &ar->txq_woken);
+	if (!READ_ONCE(ath11k_txq_wake_check)) {
+		set_bit(txq->ac, &ar->txq_woken);
+		queue_work(system_bh_wq, &ar->txq_work);
+		return;
+	}
+
+	/* Every frame mac80211 queues wakes its TXQ, from whichever CPU sent
+	 * it, and the bit operation and queue_work()'s test_and_set_bit()
+	 * are read-modify-writes of lines all of those CPUs share. They
+	 * change nothing while the AC is marked and the work has not started:
+	 * the work clears its pending bit and then, after a full barrier,
+	 * takes the marks with xchg(). The barrier here orders the frame
+	 * mac80211 queued before the reads, so a work that has started takes
+	 * the frame when it reads a mark we saw, and one we saw pending reads
+	 * our mark.
+	 */
+	smp_mb();
+	if (!test_bit(txq->ac, &ar->txq_woken)) {
+		set_bit(txq->ac, &ar->txq_woken);
+		/* the mark before the read of the pending bit */
+		smp_mb__after_atomic();
+	}
+	if (work_pending(&ar->txq_work))
+		return;
+
 	queue_work(system_bh_wq, &ar->txq_work);
 }
 
